TI  - When Shared Knowledge Hurts: Spectral Over-Accumulation in Model Merging
AU  - Yayuan Li
AU  - Ze Peng
AU  - Jian Zhang
AU  - Jintao Guo
AU  - Yue Duan
AU  - Yinghuan Shi
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2026)
AB  - Model merging combines multiple fine-tuned models into a single model by $\textitadding$ their weight updates, providing a lightweight alternative to retraining. Existing methods primarily target resolving conflicts between task updates, leaving the failure mode of over-counting shared knowledge unaddressed. We show that when tasks share aligned spectral directions (i.e., overlapping singular vectors), a simple linear combination repeatedly accumulates these directions, inflating the singular values and biasing the merged model toward shared subspaces. To mitigate this issue, we propose Singular Value Calibration (SVC), a training-free and data-free post-processing method that quantifies subspace overlap and rescales inflated singular values to restore a balanced spectrum. Across vision and language benchmarks, SVC consistently improves strong merging baselines and achieves state-of-the-art performance. Furthermore, by modifying only the singular values, SVC improves the performance of Task Arithmetic by 13.0\%. Code is available at https://github.com/lyymuwu/SVC.
